d1-Omni-2B Decide Android sample

Model: d1-Omni-2B by Liquid AI, Inc. (huggingface.co/LiquidAI/d1-Omni-2B, revision
431f1d667de5afcc9b0c58b2d9c170a7b38b358b), licensed under the LFM Open License v1.0 (LICENSE,
unchanged). The LiteRT graphs, the embedding table and the provider files the app loads come from
the model repository litert-community/d1-Omni-2B-LiteRT, a converted and modified Derivative Work of
that model whose NOTICE lists the changes. They are copied to the phone, not kept in this source tree.

The host is a Kotlin port of the Python code that ships with the model (same revision, LFM Open
License v1.0), as the model repository's host (host/d1_host.py, host/d1_audio_features.py) runs it,
so the app builds the same prompts, the same clip features and reads the same answers as the
model's own code; each ported file carries a notice that it was ported and changed:
- Prompt.kt: prompt.py, grid.py and parts of attention.py and media.py (a spoken question's place)
- Answers.kt: api.py, box.py, calibration.py, json_spans.py and parts of pointer.py
- Parsing.kt: parsing.py
- Host.kt: the sequence packing of attention.py, the slot reading of runner.py, the pointer reads
  of pointer.py, the readouts of prompt.py and the media markup of media.Reader
- Pictures.kt: smart_resize of the model's image processor (image_processing_lfm3_vl.py)
- Audio.kt: media.read_audio and media.resampled, and the vendored Lfm3OmniFeatureExtractor
Changes: Kotlin instead of Python; one request per call with at most one picture; WAV files are read
as 16-bit PCM only; a clip at a rate whose resampling filter would exceed 1,000,000 values is refused;
the language model, the picture tower and the audio encoder run as LiteRT graphs (the conversion in
litert-community/d1-Omni-2B-LiteRT), on the GPU at FP32 or, for the decision graph on a Galaxy S26,
on its NPU, with the embedding rows looked up on the host and the output rows read only for each
question's options.

Audio.kt follows the float32 arithmetic of the libraries the provider's code runs on:
- the mel filters of transformers' audio_utils.mel_filter_bank (Apache License 2.0,
  licenses/Transformers-APACHE-2.0.txt): https://github.com/huggingface/transformers
- the real FFT of pocketfft as PyTorch runs it (BSD-3-Clause, Copyright (C) 2010-2019
  Max-Planck-Society, licenses/pocketfft-BSD-3-Clause.txt): https://gitlab.mpcdf.mpg.de/mtr/pocketfft
- the float32 sine, cosine and base-10 logarithm of SLEEF (Boost Software License 1.0,
  licenses/SLEEF-BSL-1.0.txt): https://github.com/shibatch/sleef
- PyTorch's periodic Hann window and strided convolution (BSD-3-Clause,
  licenses/PyTorch-BSD-3-Clause.txt): https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch

Tokenizer.kt implements byte-level BPE as the Hugging Face tokenizers library does (Apache License
2.0, licenses/Tokenizers-APACHE-2.0.txt): https://github.com/huggingface/tokenizers

Pictures.kt ports PyTorch's antialiased bicubic resize of uint8 images
(aten/src/ATen/native/cpu/UpSampleKernel.cpp), BSD-3-Clause (licenses/PyTorch-BSD-3-Clause.txt):
https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch

Recorder.kt records the microphone at 16 kHz mono 16-bit PCM and plays clips back, adapted from this
repository's d1omni sample (D1Recorder.kt, D1AudioPlayer.kt).

app/src/main/assets/beeps.wav: the recording of the source model card (three 880 Hz beeps over quiet
noise, made by the card's own numpy code), written as a 16 kHz 16-bit PCM WAV file by
scripts/make_beeps.py.

app/src/main/assets/q_beeps.wav and q_speech.wav: speech synthesised with Kokoro-82M
(hexgrad/Kokoro-82M, revision f3ff3571791e39611d31c381e3a41a3af07b4987, Apache License 2.0,
licenses/Kokoro-82M-APACHE-2.0.txt) in the voice af_heart, from the questions "How many beeps are
there?" and "Does someone speak in the recording?"; copied unchanged from the model repository's
check set (fixtures/media/).

app/src/main/assets/shop_voicemail.wav: a voicemail to an invented bike shop, speech synthesised with
Kokoro-82M (same revision and license) in the voice am_adam, from a text written for this sample ("Hi, I picked
up my bike from you on Friday, and I was charged twice on my card. Could you refund one of the payments?
Thanks."); 16 kHz 16-bit PCM, 6.81 s.

app/src/main/assets/shop_voicemail_radio.wav: another voicemail synthesised with Kokoro-82M (same revision and
license) in the voice af_sarah, from a text written for this sample ("Hello, the back brake on my bike has
stopped working. Could I bring it in on Monday morning? Please call me back."), mixed at the same loudness with
17.8 s to 26.4 s of the song "Frozen Whispers" by szegvari (https://freesound.org/people/szegvari/sounds/732895/,
Creative Commons 0 1.0 https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/; its page says it was generated with
Suno AI), cut from the sound's HQ MP3 preview; the mix then passed as a whole through a telephone band (300 Hz
to 3,400 Hz, resampled to 8 kHz and back to 16 kHz); 16 kHz 16-bit PCM, 8.6 s.

The shop, its record and the callers of the voicemail examples are invented. The questions and states of
app/src/main/assets/examples.json were written for this sample.

Gradle wrapper files retain their upstream Apache License 2.0 headers.
